-- Caves — assertions about a playfield bigger than the screen.
--
-- These are written in the relative vocabulary for the usual reason: the level
-- is 60x30 on every console, but the *view* is 20x18 on a Game Boy and 40x28 on
-- a Mega Drive, so anything said in absolute screen terms would only be true on
-- one machine. `levelwidth`/`screenwidth` say it once and mean it everywhere.
--
-- Every case opens with `press a`, because every game opens on its title
-- screen. That is one line of ceremony in exchange for the title screen
-- being part of what the suite checks rather than something it routes
-- around: a game that never reached `play` would fail every case below.

test the hero falls onto the cavern floor and stays there
press a
play 4 seconds
expect player.ydirection <= 0.05
expect player.bottom = levelheight - 2

test the camera starts clamped into the bottom-left corner
press a
play 2 ticks
expect camera.x = 0
expect camera.y = levelheight - screenheight

test running right scrolls the view, and never past the level
press a
hold right for 2 seconds
expect camera.x > 0
expect camera.x <= levelwidth - screenwidth

-- Both halves of the HUD ride the camera, and the label has to be pinned as
-- surely as the number is — a counter that keeps up with the view while its
-- caption is left behind in the cave is worse than neither.
test the hud follows the camera rather than the world
press a
hold right for 2 seconds
expect scoretag.x = camera.x + 1
expect scoretag.y = camera.y + 1
expect score.x = camera.x + 8
expect score.y = camera.y + 1

test the spike pit is fatal, whichever console you fall into it on
press a
hold right for 5 seconds
expect scene gameover

-- The climb is a staircase whose step is the jump: five cells of rise, of which
-- the top one is spent getting *above* the ledge, so a jump that fell short of
-- five would make most of the cavern scenery. Measured just past the apex,
-- which is 0.625 seconds after the press on every console — gravity is a delta
-- a rule adds every tick, so `caves.dmt` writes it against `fps` and a machine
-- that ticks faster falls harder per second and jumps exactly as high. Written
-- as a constant it did not: the WonderSwan cleared four cells and the climb
-- died two thirds of the way up the cavern, on that console alone.
--
-- The impulse carries half a tick of gravity for the same reason, and this is
-- where the need for it showed. A tick pulls before it moves, so the first one
-- of a jump is already slowed and a slower machine loses more of the rise —
-- which put the Virtual Boy, at 50.2 ticks a second, a hundredth of a cell short
-- of the ledge while every other console cleared it by six thousandths. The
-- correction is one term and it collapses the spread tenfold.
test a jump from the floor clears five rows and not six
press a
play 4 seconds
press a
play 0.625 seconds
expect player.bottom <= levelheight - 7
expect player.bottom > levelheight - 8

test the hero cannot jump again in mid air
press a
play 4 seconds
press a
play 0.33 seconds
press a
play 2 ticks
expect player.ydirection > -1

-- The whole reason the cavern's coins are objects rather than tiles: a collected
-- one is gone. As tiles they stayed drawn and scored again every time the hero
-- re-entered the cell, which is a counter nobody can trust.
--
-- The script is portable because everything in it is absolute — an eleven-cell-
-- a-second hero on a sixty-by-thirty level takes the same ticks to reach the
-- same ledge on every console — so this is one climb, checked seven times.
test a collected coin is gone for good
press a
play 4 seconds
hold right for 0.7 seconds
press a
play 0.3 seconds
hold left for 0.43 seconds
play 1 second
expect score.value = 1
expect c1.visible = 0
play 2 seconds
expect score.value = 1
